// visio_schema/v1/service/diag/diag.proto
//
// Diagnostic-log read service: pull the device's on-flash log off a SEALED
// unit. Host→device requests ride the CONTROL_STREAM_DIAG control stream
// (end-to-end: forwarded across hops, so DiagRequest carries target_device,
// exactly like Command and OtaMessage). Device→host replies are published on
// the per-device named channel "/<device>/diag" (a dynamic data channel
// advertised via DeviceInfo — NOT a reserved control id), mirroring the
// OtaMessage (control id) / "/ota_status" (named channel) split.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. A customer image binds no adbd, and no Command or setup-page
// route returns a file, so until now a sealed unit had no read path at all: the
// only evidence a returned device carried was whatever the customer remembered.
// The device now keeps a bounded encrypted ring of its own log
// (docs/protocol/diag_log.md); this is how the app collects it during an RMA
// or a one-tap "report a problem", and how the bench reads it at incoming
// inspection.
//
// Why its own control stream rather than a Command body: a read is a chunked
// transfer of up to a few MB, which is the shape OTA already has, inverted —
// the device is the sender. It does not fit the single-ack CommandResult model.
//
// The bytes are opaque to the host: a `.vdlg` file is ciphertext under a fleet
// key the app never holds, and the host relays it unchanged into RMA evidence.
// A dev image with no key staged writes the ring in the clear, and the same
// transfer carries that too.
//
// Throttling is the DEVICE's job. A read must never disturb a live recording;
// the device paces chunks against its own I/O and the host simply consumes.

